      "side": 1,
      "message": "Now that the compatible strings are not nested under named-temp-sensors, one could add more temperature sensors to the devicetree than children defined under \u0027named-temp-sensor\u0027.\n\n```\n\u0026i2c0 {\n    tmp112_cpu: tmp112@40 {};\n    tmp112_ddr: tmp112@42 {};\n};\nnamed-temp-sensors {\n    cpu_temp: cpu-temp { sensor \u003d \u003c\u0026tmp112_cpu\u003e; };\n};\n```\n\nFor the above setting devicetree, TEMP_SENSOR_COUNT will be 2, but TEMP_SENSOR_COUNT of 1 is expected.  This will also cause a mismatch in the array sizes of `temp_sensors[]` and `thermal_params[]`.  The thermal code assumes these arrays are the same size.\n\nBy switching to DT_FOREACH_CHILD, TEMP_SENSOR_COUNT will always match the number of children under the `cros-ec,temp-sensors` node.\n\nThe tricky part is to assigning the `temp_sensors[].zephyr_temp_sensor` pointer correctly based on the `sensor` property phandle.  I think this is doable by using FOREACH_STATUS_OKAY  on each sensor type to declare the `zephyr_temp_sensor`.\n\nThe other thing to look at which node label should be used with `TEMP_SENSOR_ID()`.  From the example above, your current code uses `TEMP_SENSOR_ID(DT_NODELABEL(tmp112_cpu))`, but I think it makes sense to use the child nodelabel instead: `TEMP_SENSOR_ID(DT_NODELABEL(cpu_temp))`.  What do you think?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 39,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 39,
        "endChar": 65
      },
      "revId": "fe40b75a62440640a7b63f0e0add2bd911ca57a9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
